-0.0338134765625, -0.01247406005859375, -0.02374267578125, 0.00983428955078125, 0.015777587890625, -0.004058837890625, 0.0232391357421875, 0.0173187255859375, -0.01165771484375, -0.00531768798828125, 0.0135498046875, -0.0025157928466796875, 0.03216552734375, -0.00017762184143066406, 0.00925445556640625, -0.0101165771484375, 0.039093017578125, 0.01374053955078125, 0.0107421875, 0.017333984375, -0.02142333984375, -0.010223388671875, 0.01021575927734375, -0.01093292236328125, -0.02667236328125, 0.0328369140625, 0.00466156005859375, 0.041656494140625, -0.0167999267578125, 0.01515960693359375, -0.0007810592651367188, -0.00710296630859375, 0.0164031982421875, -0.0080413818359375, 0.01457977294921875, -0.01448822021484375, -0.0220794677734375, 0.03717041015625, -0.0308837890625, -0.01546478271484375, 0.012176513671875, -0.003055572509765625, 0.0156707763671875, 0.01318359375, 0.054168701171875, 0.041290283203125, -0.00899505615234375, -0.0132293701171875, 0.004467010498046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: The aim of this study was to develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance. The goal is to support standardized reporting in clinical practice, thereby reducing errors and improving patient care in radiology.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. The multi-agent AI pipeline consisted of three sequential agents: (1) a structuring agent that segmented reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level using regex rules and local large language models; (2) a consistency-checking agent that detected mismatches between the Findings and Impression sections or within sections; and (3) a QA agent that identified gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented communication of critical findings using rule-based and language model prompts. Each agent operated independently, passing its output to the next agent in sequence. No automated feedback loops or error correction between agents were implemented in this version; this modular approach was chosen to facilitate error tracing and allow for independent evaluation of each agent\u2019s performance, which is a common strategy in modular AI system design.\n\nFor radiologist evaluation, a random subset of 45 reports was selected using stratified random sampling to represent the distribution of body regions (chest, abdomen, pelvis) and the types of flagged issues (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, critical findings) present in the full dataset. Stratification was performed to ensure that the sample reflected the proportions of each body region and flagged issue type observed in the overall dataset, though exact proportions were matched as closely as possible given the sample size. Two board-certified radiologists independently reviewed these reports. \u201cCorrectly restructured\u201d was defined as accurate anatomical section assignment for the entire report without altering original content; \u201cincorrectly restructured\u201d indicated misassignment or loss of information at the report level; \u201cdisagreement\u201d reflected differing reviewer assessments per report. In cases of disagreement, no consensus process was used; this approach was chosen to capture independent reviewer perspectives and to avoid introducing bias that can occur when reviewers are required to reach consensus, though it may limit reliability by not resolving differences.\n\nInter-rater agreement was calculated using Cohen\u2019s kappa.\n\nResults: The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content. The system flagged 90 of 638 reports (14.1%, 95% CI: 11.5\u201317.0%) for at least one issue, including section mismatches (80 of 638 reports, 12.5%, 95% CI: 10.1\u201315.4%), gender-anatomy conflicts (5 of 638 reports, 0.8%), and undocumented communication of critical findings (3 of 638 reports, 0.5%). In the radiologist evaluation, both reviewers agreed that 31 of 45 reports (69%, 95% CI: 53\u201382%) were correctly restructured, 2 of 45 reports (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and there was disagreement on 12 of 45 reports (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201342%). Cohen\u2019s kappa for agreement was 0.62, which is generally interpreted as substantial agreement (values between 0.61 and 0.80 are considered substantial). Both reviewers independently assessed whether any clinically important information was omitted or any fabricated content was introduced, using an open-ended review of each report; both agreed that neither occurred. Overall QA performance was rated as \"excellent\" in 24 of 45 reports (53%), \"good\" in 14 of 45 reports (31%), and \"fair\" in 7 of 45 reports (16%), corresponding to 38 of 45 reports (84%, 95% CI: 70\u201393%) rated as \"excellent\" or \"good\".\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system combined radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow, demonstrating favorable performance in radiologist evaluation.\n\nLimitations:\n- Retrospective design, which may introduce selection or information bias\n- Single-institution setting\n- Limited sample size for radiologist evaluation\n- Potential sources of error such as misclassification of anatomical sections or missed inconsistencies\n- Reviewer selection may introduce bias, as only board-certified radiologists from the same institution participated\n- Generalizability to other institutions, imaging modalities, or report types remains to be established\n\nFuture work will focus on expanding the system to additional report types, incorporating automated feedback mechanisms between agents, and further improving error detection.\n\nCompliance: The study was conducted with institutional review board approval and in compliance with data privacy regulations; all data were anonymized and stored on secure, access-controlled servers.\n\nSuch systems may support standardization of reporting and quality assurance in radiology practice, with the potential to streamline workflows and improve clinical communication.\n\nReference:\nLandis JR, Koch GG. The measurement of observer agreement for categorical data. Biometrics. 1977;33(1):159-174."}}